  "why": "Sequelize as its own example app writes it: `sequelize.define` inside a function the connection is handed to, a class extending Sequelize's `Model` with columns from `init`, and associations in a separate file on locals destructured from the models registry \u2014 a file that never imports `sequelize` itself. REFERENCES follows the foreign key, and each association kind has an edge of its own. The model reached through an import has a *different name at every level* \u2014 the class `Musician`, the model `musician` (from `modelName`), and the local `Player` \u2014 so reading any of the three as the model's name fails the case. `seed.js` holds three `define` calls in a file that imports `sequelize` and none is a model: an untyped map, and two values rooted at Sequelize (`Op.is`, `Sequelize.NOW`) that are not column types. Columns use the parameterized `STRING(80)` form too.",
